 failures and i'm  hoping to continue it as you go along  with the series  basically looking at a different person  and see how they failed early on and how  you can learn from that success so today  i want to talk about marilyn monroe  now marilyn monroe  obviously one of the most you know  celebrated actresses ever in the in the  world and when she had her first  contract when she was super young  in 1947 she had her first acting  contract she's with 20th century fox  they gave her one year contract and the  production chief daryl zenik at the time  refused to  continue on with the deal and actually  let her go  because he said that she was  unattractive  marilyn monroe  one of the sexiest women on the planet  ever  was told  that she was unattractive  and was fired from her contract  think about that  one of my  one of my things is you know these guys  i call them the little man okay so daryl  zanik this production chief he's the  little man you're gonna have the little  man in your life the people who don't  believe in you the people who  tell you you're unattractive  when you're not and tell your ideas  stupid when it's not who just can't see  the vision that you see  and you're gonna you're gonna come  across these people  a lot in your life  and sometimes  they're your friends sometimes they're  your family  and you know what i find it often it's  easier to get criticism from your  friends and family because you can you  can always say you know what they don't  know what they're talking about  they don't know what they're talking  about  you know they have no experience  but sometimes the little man knows what  he's talking about  right he's an expert  he's had years of experience  daryl zanik production chief at 20th  century fox  has had years of experience  and this is the guy telling marilyn  monroe that she's unattractive and  letting her go from her contract  so sometimes the expert they don't know  what they're saying  right they're they're basing their views  on the world that exists in their head  that may not be your reality  and so what do you do  this decision time  when the people around you tell you that  you're not going to make it the experts  in the field say you know it's a stupid  idea you're unattractive forget about it  quit leave give up  what do you do  that's when it really tests you  that's when it really tests you as to is  this the right thing for you or not  because it's not going to be an easy  pass  you're going to have to fight for it  do you really love it enough to keep  going  what if marilyn monroe had listened to  this guy and and thought to herself you  know what i'm not i'm not attractive i  can't make it as an actress  what would have happened to her life  would have been totally different than  what she got  and the same thing can happen to you if  you listen to that criticism  if you stop  because somebody tells you that it's not  a good idea but deep down in your heart  you feel like  you don't know what else to do this is  what you love  then you have to find a way to keep  going  and stop listening to the little men  i've got time for one last question i'm  going to take one from twitter we've got  a lot of questions coming in on the  twitter channel this one is from  steampressmedia  who is asking me who is my mentor who do  i look up to  my favorite entrepreneur of all time  is ap giannini  and he's the founder of bank of america  you might be saying ep janine who is  this guy and a banker what the heck man  a banker is your favorite entrepreneur  this guy is not your typical banker  okay he started off not in the banking  industry and he was asked to join the  board of directors for bank called  columbus loans and savings and he joined  as a board member and he saw that the  bank was only giving out  loans to wealthy people and he said you  know what there's all these immigrant  workers here there's all these small  business owners who  they work really hard and i think if we  give them some money they'll pay it back  and they'll be honest people  and they said knox that's crazy we can't  see that that's way too risky  so he got so upset with him that he left  the board and he opened up his own bank  right across the street  from columbus savings and loan  talk about ballsy and he started giving  out loans to hard-working people the  little man the little guys right like  the the  the hard-working immigrants and small  business owners who nobody wanted to to  give a shot to  he was the guy  and his business started to grow and it  became bank of america one of the  largest banks in the world  and one of my favorite stories  is when there was  a fire in san francisco  that totaled the whole city all the  buildings were were on fire burnt down  businesses needed money to get their  lives back in order they need to pay for  things and all the banks they shut down  because their buildings have been shut  down and what ap janine did was he went  to his bank which had been burned to the  ground he took out two barrels and he  took a plank of wood and he brought a  chair out  and he opened up shop because he knew  that these people needed money to  rebuild their lives and he didn't have  any records he has nothing people came  to him they formed a line and he made a  loan to people based on what he said a  look in their eye and a handshake deal  are you kidding me  what kind of banker imagine going to  your bank  looking them in the eye and shaking  their hand and then giving you a deal  right it doesn't happen  but that was what he believed in he  wanted to support the hard-working  entrepreneurs and give them a hand up  because sometimes we all need a little  bit of help and so that's why he's my  favorite entrepreneur he's somebody who  i've tried to model he's been my mentor  even though he died way before i was  even born  but i encourage you to look at those  stories like find somebody who you look  up to learn from them take lessons from  their success  and apply it to your business  it's been another great show thank you  guys so much for calling in uh thank you  lauren and josh for all the great work  producing the show again join me on  twitter i'm at evan carmichael and i  will see you in two weeks for the next  show
 

